INSERT INTO level VALUES(1,'First Contact','A C program is written as text, compiled into machine code, then run. You learn that edit, compile, run loop and the shape of main.','A compiler is not one step. Name the four stages gcc runs from source file to executable.','Preprocessing, compilation to assembly, assembling into an object file, then linking with libraries.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(2,'Data and Types','Variables are named boxes with a fixed type. The type decides how many bytes the box uses and how those bytes are read.','Why can unsigned u = 0; u - 1 give a huge number, while int i = INT_MAX; i + 1 is undefined behaviour?','Unsigned arithmetic wraps around by definition. Signed overflow has no defined result, so the compiler may assume it never happens.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(3,'Expressions and I/O','Operators combine values into new values, and printf and scanf move data out and in. Precedence decides which operator is applied first.','What can int x = 5; int y = x++ + ++x; evaluate to, and why is writing this a bug?','x is modified twice with no sequence point between the changes, so the behaviour is undefined and any result, even a crash, is allowed.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(4,'Decisions and Bits','if and switch let a program choose a path. Bitwise operators work on the individual 0s and 1s inside a number.','Why does the test n & (n - 1) == 0 not do what you expect for powers of two, and how do you write it correctly?','== binds tighter than &, so it parses as n & ((n - 1) == 0). Write (n & (n - 1)) == 0 and also require n > 0, because 0 would pass.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(5,'Repetition','Loops repeat work with for, while and do-while. The skill is picking the right loop and making sure it always ends.','A countdown is written for (unsigned i = n; i >= 0; i--). What goes wrong and how do you fix it?','An unsigned value is always >= 0, so the loop never ends and i wraps to a huge number. Use for (i = n; i-- > 0;) or a signed type.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(6,'Building Blocks','Functions package reusable steps and arrays hold many values of one type in a row. C passes arguments by value, so a function receives copies.','Inside main, sizeof(arr) gives the whole array size, but inside a function taking int arr[] it gives the size of a pointer. Why?','An array parameter decays into a pointer to its first element, so the length is lost. Pass the element count as a separate argument.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(7,'Meeting Memory','A pointer stores the address of another value. Strings are char arrays that end with a zero byte, and scope decides where a name can be seen.','Why is changing a character through a char pointer to a string literal undefined, while the same edit on a char array initialised from that literal works?','String literals usually sit in read-only memory. An array initialised from a literal gets its own writable copy.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(8,'Structured Memory','Structs group related fields, pointer arithmetic steps through memory in units of a type, and files keep data between runs. Function pointers let you pass behaviour around.','For struct { char a; int b; char c; } why is sizeof often 12 and not 6, and how do you shrink it to 8?','The compiler inserts padding so the int sits on a 4-byte boundary and the total is a multiple of 4. Reorder the fields as int b, char a, char c.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(9,'Heap and Build','malloc and free give you memory that outlives a function, and you must give every block back. Make rebuilds only what changed, and sorting shows how algorithm choice affects speed.','If p = realloc(p, bigger) fails and returns NULL, what has leaked and what is the safe pattern?','The old block is still allocated but you overwrote the only pointer to it. Assign to a temporary, check it for NULL, and only then update p.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(10,'Systems Craft','Linked lists build structures from separate heap nodes. gdb and Valgrind hunt bugs, fork and exec start processes, and threads run code at the same time.','Two threads each run counter++ one million times on a shared int. Why is the total usually below two million, and how do you fix it?','counter++ is a read, add and write sequence, so two threads can read the same value and one update is lost. Protect it with a mutex or use an atomic increment.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(11,'Data Structures','Stacks, queues and trees organise data for fast access. Pointers link the pieces together and recursion is a natural way to walk them.','When you delete a binary search tree node that has two children, which node can take its place and why does the ordering stay valid?','Use the in-order successor, the smallest node in the right subtree, or the in-order predecessor. It is greater than everything on the left and not greater than the rest of the right side.');
INSERT INTO level VALUES(12,'Capstone','Combine everything in one real project: a memory allocator that manages raw bytes, tracks free blocks and fights fragmentation.','How can your allocator catch a double free, and why must returned addresses be aligned, for example to 16 bytes on x86-64?','Keep a header with an in-use flag or magic value and check it in free. Alignment lets any type, including SSE vectors, be stored safely and efficiently at the returned address.');
